<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  `Networking`'s privacy manifest.

  Phase 11 item 6. Two things leave the device from this target.

  `UserRepository.updateProfile` `PATCH`es the display name, so `Name` is
  collected: linked to the account, not used for tracking, app functionality.
  The `GET` of `/users/me` is not collection — it reads data back rather than
  transmitting it — and the account deletion carries no body at all.

  `X-Attest-Key-Id` rides every request the attestor could sign, which is the
  App Attest key identifier out of the Keychain. Declaring that as `DeviceID`
  is a judgement call and the narrower reading is defensible: the key is
  per-install, it is destroyed by a reinstall, and it cannot be correlated
  across apps the way an IDFV can. It is declared anyway, because the cost of
  over-declaring here is one more row in a privacy report and the cost of
  under-declaring is a manifest that says the app sends no identifier while it
  sends one on every request. See `docs/privacy-manifest.md`.

  Phase 11 item 7 adds the third and fourth rows. `APICrashReportUploader`
  `POST`s a MetricKit-derived report to `/diagnostics/reports`, which is
  `CrashData` for the crash diagnostics and `PerformanceData` for the hang, CPU,
  disk-write and launch ones — two rows rather than one because they are two of
  Apple's categories and a reader of the privacy report is entitled to know
  which of them this app sends.

  **Both are declared `Linked`, and that is the judgement call.** A report carries
  no account identifier and the request is deliberately unauthenticated — a crash
  before sign-in is the one most worth having. But the transport attaches
  `X-Attest-Key-Id` to every request it can sign, including this one, so the
  report arrives beside a per-install identifier that is already declared as
  `DeviceID` two rows up. Claiming the diagnostics are unlinked while sending them
  next to an identifier would be a false statement; the cost of the honest answer
  is one column in a privacy report.

  The purpose is `AppFunctionality` rather than `Analytics`: these reports exist
  to fix defects in the app, not to measure what anybody does with it. Nothing
  here aggregates them into behaviour, and `NSPrivacyTracking` stays false
  because none of it is joined to data from anywhere else.

  What is *not* sent is as much the point: `docs/crash-reporting.md` lists the
  fields the projection drops — `MXFrame.address`, `virtualMemoryRegionInfo` and
  `MXMetaData.regionFormat` — and why a manifest cannot state any of it.

  No required-reason API: nothing in this target reads `UserDefaults`, a file
  timestamp, the boot time, free disk space or the active keyboards. The token
  store is Keychain-only, which is not a required-reason category.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePerformanceData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
